// induction: Gauss sum, n(n+1)/2
theorem: for all n in N, sum from i=1 to n of i = n(n+1)/2
proof
we proceed by induction on n.
base case n = 0: sum from i=1 to 0 of i = 0 = 0(0+1)/2.
inductive step: assume sum from i=1 to k of i = k(k+1)/2.
then sum from i=1 to k+1 of i = k(k+1)/2 + (k+1) = (k+1)(k+2)/2.
qed
